{"authored" [{"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x})+9(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}) & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{12}e^x+\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{4}e^x+e^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8xe^{5x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=4{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "f405a87e1a556feea9d0c73ef40350f39ecb9f5d847533bb85d55b7ac35379ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21615.json" "file_sha256" "b26c3aec81b7d61b80567135133560bd6620429883615045f8b1efb796e5b9ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21615.json" "file_sha256" "b26c3aec81b7d61b80567135133560bd6620429883615045f8b1efb796e5b9ca" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21621.json" "file_sha256" "ac147dec3b472c4e202a93344a3f3b9168d5739c310a5f9e8b74577e993f5074" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21621.json" "file_sha256" "ac147dec3b472c4e202a93344a3f3b9168d5739c310a5f9e8b74577e993f5074" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 6 y = 9 e ^ {6 x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{6x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21616.json" "file_sha256" "9d3439fece8c9430525ddbd0d8452a51d42fdfc1253b2c97f599a2691726d723" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21616" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6 y_c=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9 e ^ {6 x},$ and $e^{6x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {6x} ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of $y_p$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{6x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{6x}+6x{e}^{6x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+6x){e}^{6x})-6\\alpha x{e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ (1+6x-6x)\\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{6x} & =9{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 9x  e ^ {6 x} .\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{6x} + 9xe^{6x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21616.json" "file_sha256" "9d3439fece8c9430525ddbd0d8452a51d42fdfc1253b2c97f599a2691726d723" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}, \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' - 2y = 4e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21617.json" "file_sha256" "8bd78d0e31389978a988844a130114e8f80ca126de17eee4c5ff896f2eabee98" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-21617" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $4e^{3x},$ and $e^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' = 3\\alpha e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3\\alpha {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{3x}) & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ (3-2)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =4{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 4e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+4 & =1 \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = -3e^{2x} + 4e ^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21617.json" "file_sha256" "8bd78d0e31389978a988844a130114e8f80ca126de17eee4c5ff896f2eabee98" "basis" 1347 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-333160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d44fc3d7528862a9a2723c609b8e04bddc5544f7fc729124ca96f67101e04" "basis" 1676 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-333160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d44fc3d7528862a9a2723c609b8e04bddc5544f7fc729124ca96f67101e04" "basis" 1676 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-333160" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1aef8361c78410ac53be90a1280497511be73fd3220b98943603a6a7db5f399a" "basis" 1676 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-333160" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d44fc3d7528862a9a2723c609b8e04bddc5544f7fc729124ca96f67101e04" "basis" 1676 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-333160" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(12x+1)e^{-3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff2d44fc3d7528862a9a2723c609b8e04bddc5544f7fc729124ca96f67101e04" "basis" 1676 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045844/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-332659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-18y=24{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $24{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x})-18(\\alpha {e}^{-2x}) & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (-12-18)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -30\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =24{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332659" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332659" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332659" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{5}e^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x},$ and neither ${e}^{2x}$ nor ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x}+5\\beta {e}^{5x})+3(\\alpha {e}^{2x}+\\beta {e}^{5x}) & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ (2\\alpha +3\\alpha ){e}^{2x}+(5\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x} \\\\ 5\\alpha {e}^{2x}+8\\beta {e}^{5x} & =2{e}^{2x}+{e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{5x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n5\\alpha =2,\\,8\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332669" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5}e^{2x}+\\frac{1}{8}e^{5x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=9{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $9{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4\\alpha {e}^{4x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{4x}) & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ (12-6)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ 6\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =9{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}e^{4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(\\alpha {e}^{x}+4\\beta {e}^{4x})+9(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{4x}) & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ (3\\alpha +9\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(12\\beta +9\\beta ){e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x} \\\\ 12\\alpha {e}^{x}+21\\beta {e}^{4x} & =7{e}^{x}+4{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{4x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n12\\alpha =7\\,\\text{and}\\,21\\beta =4,\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\frac{7}{12}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{21}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{7}{12}{e}^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{12}e^{x}+\\frac{4}{21}e^{4x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x},$ and neither ${e}^{x}$ nor ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})-6(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2\\alpha -6\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(4\\beta -6\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x} \\\\ -4\\alpha {e}^{x}-2\\beta {e}^{2x} & =3{e}^{x}-2{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{x}$ and ${e}^{2x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha =3,\\,\\,-2\\beta =-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\,\\beta =1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332670" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{4}e^{x}+e^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332728" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8xe^{5x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{-4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-4x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-4x}-4x{e}^{-4x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-4x){e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+4(\\alpha x{e}^{-4x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-4x+4x)\\alpha {e}^{-4x}+(\\beta +4\\beta ){e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-4x}+5\\beta {e}^{x} & =12{e}^{-4x}+8{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-4x}$ and ${e}^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =8,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =12\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-4x}+\\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2f9099d0d7d328ac6c30752ac6775ff692eef50003b822784b4e0bebc822607e"} {"question" "q-332848" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073017/state.json" "file_sha256" "98a6286183045773c303272daf8f2e8e6fab516e2b387d7979a82dea1c91b877"} {"question" "q-332848" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12xe^{-4x}+\\